  "flow": "Execution does not follow the numbered roles. The declaration transformer drives type serialization: when it needs a type for an inferred declaration it calls into the resolver/node builder, which reaches role 1 symbolToTypeNode (nodebuilderimpl.go:650). Role 1 calls the adapter lookupSymbolChain (nodebuilderimpl.go:1061), whose first act is role 3 TrackSymbol (nodebuilderimpl.go:1062). Role 3 excludes type parameters and consumes the class-expression watch, then asks the resolver (emitresolver.go:685 adapter) which reaches role 2 isSymbolAccessibleWorker (symbolaccessibility.go:843). Role 2 first delegates chain discovery to IsAnySymbolAccessible (getAccessibleSymbolChain + hasVisibleDeclarations; emitresolver.go:384-465) and only on failure distinguishes an unnameable foreign-module symbol (CannotBeNamed, via getExternalModuleContainer) from an inaccessible local one (NotAccessible). Role 2's result flows back into role 4 handleSymbolAccessibilityError (tracker.go:204), which either queues newly visible aliases onto state.lateMarkedStatements or emits a context-dependent diagnostic via state.getSymbolAccessibilityDiagnostic/errorNameNode (installed by setupDiagnosticContext, transform.go:551). So role 1 -> adapter lookupSymbolChain -> role 3 -> adapter EmitResolver.IsSymbolAccessible -> role 2 -> role 4. A parallel path, transform.go:1712 checkEntityNameVisibility, calls EmitResolver.IsEntityNameVisible (emitresolver.go:334) -> hasVisibleDeclarations and then calls role 4 directly. Later, role 5 transformAndReplaceLatePaintedStatements (transform.go:386) drains the same worklist and re-invokes transformTopLevelDeclaration; that re-transform can call role 1 again, closing the loop. The alias worklist is state.lateMarkedStatements (tracker.go:240), the replacement map is tx.lateStatementReplacementMap (transform.go:80, initialized 294) keyed by original-node identity ast.GetNodeId(MostOriginal(node)) (transform.go:415-417, 428-430), diagnostic context is getSymbolAccessibilityDiagnostic/errorNameNode, and import-mode state is importModeOverride together with GetModuleResolutionKind/GetEmitModuleFormatOfFile (nodebuilderimpl.go:669-705).\n\nWhy draining only the aliases present at the start leaves stale output: the worklist is monotonic and self-feeding. Checking D's visibility marks C visible and enqueues it; re-transforming C marks B, and so on (the comment's module A {Q; import B=Q; import C=B; export import D=C} example, transform.go:389-400). Statements were already transformed once in the first visit pass (visitDeclarationStatements stores a replacement, transform.go:1182-1188), but that form was computed under incomplete visibility. If we drained only the initial queue, aliases first made visible during that drain would never be re-transformed, so their earlier output forms would remain in lateStatementReplacementMap and be emitted stale (or the references would name an alias that is never emitted). The for/while loop re-checks len(lateMarkedStatements)==0 each iteration precisely to reach a fixed point, and transformTopLevelDeclaration removes the node from the queue (1719-1722) to avoid duplicate processing.\n\nReplacement outcomes: in the final pass, a missing map entry means no replacement (original statement kept, 431-433); a nil replacement means deletion (435-437); a SyntaxList replacement means expansion into several statements (438-449), with scope-marker/external-module recomputation; anything else is a single statement replacement (450-458).\n\nWhy unresolved names avoid an extra accessibility diagnostic: role 4 only reports when Accessibility != SymbolAccessibilityNotResolved. NotResolved comes from isEntityNameVisible when the first identifier cannot be resolved (emitresolver.go:362-368); the checker already issues an unresolvable-name error, so declaring an additional 'inferred type references inaccessible type' error would be redundant (comment at tracker.go:214-215).\n\nWhen retrying module spelling with a different resolution mode prevents a portability error: in role 1, a specifier containing '/node_modules/' is non-portable in Node16/NodeNext. The code swaps the emit module mode (ESNext<->CommonJS) and regenerates the specifier; if the new spelling avoids '/node_modules/', it sets importModeOverride to that mode (nodebuilderimpl.go:683-697) and no error is reported. Only if the retry still contains '/node_modules/' does it restore the original and call ReportLikelyUnsafeImportRequiredError (699-704), producing the 'This is likely not portable' diagnostic (tracker.go:103-112).\n\nResulting invariants: (a) every serialized type reference must name an accessible symbol either directly, through a retained/marked-visible internal alias, or through an import(\"...\") type; (b) aliases used to name types are marked visible and re-transformed to a fixed point; (c) output replacement is keyed on original-node identity in lateStatementReplacementMap; (d) statements resolve to no-replacement, deletion, or SyntaxList expansion consistently; (e) unresolved names are reported once by the checker, not duplicated by declaration emit; (f) node_modules-diving import types are converted via mode override or reported as non-portable. Limits of this revision: the dependent/cascading alias-visibility marking only works for internal import aliases (transform.go:400: 'Today, this dependent alias marking only happens for internal import aliases'); visibility restoration in hasVisibleDeclarations only covers a fixed set of shapes (unexported import syntax, unexported variable statements, IsLateVisibilityPaintedStatement, and binding elements; emitresolver.go:404-454); IsSymbolAccessibleByFlags deliberately disables modules while IsSymbolAccessible allows them (symbolaccessibility.go:22,839-840); and portability handling is gated to Node16/NodeNext so other resolution modes can still name types through node_modules paths."
