{
  "findings": [
    {
      "path": "tsc/internal/ls/rename.go",
      "symbol": "symbolAndEntriesToRename",
      "line": 98,
      "explanation": "This is the routine that validates the original node and turns the already-resolved symbol-and-reference results into a WorkspaceEdit. It first rejects ineligible nodes via nodeIsEligibleForRename(data.OriginalNode) and, as defense-in-depth for clients that skip prepareRename, re-runs getRenameInfoForNode on the original node, returning an empty edit if the node cannot be renamed (lines 99-111). It then flattens data.SymbolsAndEntries into a single []*ReferenceEntry list (113), obtains the type checker and quote preference/useAliasesForRename from user preferences (115-119), and iterates each reference. For each entry it computes an LSP range with renameEditRange and, when that range is not exactly mappable back to the original text (ok == false), it skips that occurrence while continuing with the rest (126-131). Otherwise it builds a TextEdit whose NewText comes from getTextForRename and appends it to mappedEdits (132-136). Finally it calls deduplicateRenameEdits; if dedup reports conflicting replacement text it aborts with an empty WorkspaceEdit (138-141), otherwise it returns a WorkspaceEdit carrying Changes keyed by document URI (142-146)."
    },
    {
      "path": "tsc/internal/ls/rename.go",
      "symbol": "getTextForRename",
      "line": 368,
      "explanation": "This helper chooses the actual replacement text for one occurrence based on the relationship between the renamed symbol and that occurrence (entry.kind) and on the syntactic form of the occurrence. It only applies prefix/suffix rewriting when useAliasesForRename is set, the entry is not a plain range, and the original node is an identifier or string literal (369). It reparses the reference node and inspects its parent. For a shorthand property assignment or a non-rest object binding element whose name is the reference (376), the occurrence denotes both a property and a value, so it must become an explicit rewrite: when the search started at the local symbol and found the property (entryKindSearchedLocalFoundProperty) it emits \"name: newText\" (377-379); when the search started at the property and found the local symbol (entryKindSearchedPropertyFoundLocal) it emits \"newText: name\" (380-382); otherwise it disambiguates module.exports shorthand and defaults to \"newText: name\" (385-392). For import/export specifiers without a property name (393, 405) it decides whether to keep the original as an alias, producing forms like \"name as newText\" or \"newText as name\", based on whether the original symbol/declaration is this occurrence's symbol (394-410). A numeric index literal on an access expression is quoted (415-418). In all other cases it simply returns newText (420)."
    },
    {
      "path": "tsc/internal/ls/rename.go",
      "symbol": "deduplicateRenameEdits",
      "line": 44,
      "explanation": "This helper consolidates the collected mappedRenameEdit values by document and range. It keys a map of renameEditKey{uri, Range} to the NewText already seen (45-48). If a later edit targets the same document and range with identical text, it is dropped as a harmless duplicate (49-54); if it targets the same key with different text, the function returns (nil, false), signaling a genuine conflict that must abort the whole rename (50-52). Surviving unique edits are then grouped by URI into the map[DocumentUri][]*TextEdit returned to the caller (58-62). This is what distinguishes duplicate edits (same range, same replacement — collapse to one) from conflicting replacements (same range, different replacement — fail the rename)."
    }
  ],
  "flow": "ProvideRename routes through handleCrossProject, which invokes symbolAndEntriesToRename for each project and combines the results with combineRenameResponse. symbolAndEntriesToRename is the orchestrator: it validates eligibility of the original node twice (nodeIsEligibleForRename plus a full getRenameInfoForNode check for clients that skipped prepareRename), flattens the reference entries gathered by findallreferences.go, and for every occurrence acquires an LSP range via renameEditRange and a replacement string via getTextForRename, accumulating mappedRenameEdit values (uri, TextEdit). It then hands the list to deduplicateRenameEdits, which collapses exact duplicates and reports conflicts, and returns a WorkspaceEdit{Changes}. getTextForRename is the per-occurrence policy layer: it inspects entry.kind together with the reference node's parent to decide whether a plain identifier replacement suffices. A shorthand property like { x } or an object binding element like const { x } = o is a single identifier that denotes both a property key and a value; renaming only the identifier would change both meanings at once, which is usually wrong, so the helper rewrites the occurrence into an explicit property form (\"x: newText\" or \"newText: x\") using entryKindSearchedLocalFoundProperty / entryKindSearchedPropertyFoundLocal to know which side is the variable and which is the property. Likewise import/export specifiers without a PropertyName act as aliases, so the replacement may become \"name as newText\" or \"newText as name\" depending on whether the occurrence is the aliased declaration or a use, which requires knowledge of the original symbol and declarations rather than a simple token swap. For occurrences that cannot be mapped back exactly to the original text, renameEditRange resolves the entry and, for content-mapped (transformed) files, converts entry.textRange to an LSP range strictly; if the range is not fully within a single verbatim span the fidelity is not exact and ok is false, so symbolAndEntriesToRename skips that edit (line 128-131) rather than emit an edit that cannot be applied to the original source. Finally, deduplicateRenameEdits handles multiple references describing the same text range: if the replacements are identical it keeps one (they are redundant), but if they differ it returns ok=false and the rename is abandoned, preventing ambiguous overlapping edits. This matters for shorthand/alias cases where one syntactic occurrence can yield multiple reference entries that resolve to the same range with different intended relationship text."
}

{"findings": [
  {
    "path": "tsc/internal/project/project.go",
    "symbol": "CreateProgram",
    "line": 499,
    "explanation": "Project-level entry point that attempts the incremental optimization. When exactly one file is dirty (p.dirtyFilePath != \"\"), a Program already exists, and the command line/config object is identical (p.Program.CommandLine() == commandLine), it calls p.Program.UpdateProgram(p.dirtyFilePath, p.host, ...) to try a single-file clone. It manages ownership of parsed files via the parse/content-mapped refcount caches: on success (programCloned) it marks updateKind ProgramUpdateKindCloned and, because UpdateProgram only acquired the dirty file, iterates newProgram.SourceFiles() and refs every file whose pointer is not dirtyFile (skipping content-mapper failure stubs and supplementals), plus DuplicateSourceFiles(); on fallback (not cloned but dirtyFile != nil) it Derefs the speculatively acquired dirtyFile so the freshly rebuilt program is the only owner; if the optimization was never attempted it builds compiler.NewProgram. The condition mirrors the exactly-one-file/config-unchanged gate set by markFilesChanged (deleted files and package.json force dirtyFilePath to \"\")."
  },
  {
    "path": "tsc/internal/compiler/program.go",
    "symbol": "ReuseProgram",
    "line": 347,
    "explanation": "Compiler routine that tries to replace only changedFilePath in place while reusing the rest of the existing program. It takes oldFile := p.filesByPath[changedFilePath], then acquires the new file speculatively: content-mapped files re-run the host transform (with supplemental files); otherwise newHost.GetSourceFile(oldFile.ParseOptions()). It bails to (nil, newFile, false) if the path is in p.redirectFilesByPath or p.redirectTargetsMap, if p.moduleResolutionError != nil, if !p.canReplaceFileInProgram(oldFile, newFile), if either the old or new file requires an importHelpers tslib import or a JSX runtime import (bookkeeping cloning does not recompute), or if supplemental-file paths/metadata differ. On success it clones Program reusing processedFiles, cloned resolutionData, unresolvedImports/knownSymlinks/packageNames, swaps the file in the cloned files slice and filesByPath, re-inits the checker pool, and returns (result, newFile, true). UpdateProgram (line 323) wraps this, returning the reused program or falling back to NewProgram with the same newFile."
  },
  {
    "path": "tsc/internal/compiler/program.go",
    "symbol": "canReplaceFileInProgram",
    "line": 462,
    "explanation": "Predicate comparing the old and new file's structure-affecting metadata to decide whether in-place replacement is safe. It requires file2 != nil and equality of ParseOptions, ScriptKind, ast.IsExternalOrCommonJSModule, UsesUriStyleNodeCoreModules, Imports (both module-specifier text/kind via equalModuleSpecifiers and resolution mode via p.GetModeForUsageLocation), ModuleAugmentations (names), AmbientModuleNames, ReferencedFiles, TypeReferenceDirectives, LibReferenceDirectives (equalFileReferences compares FileName/ResolutionMode/Preserve), and CheckJsDirective. Any change in import specifiers, import-vs-require resolution mode, module augmentation/ambient module names, triple-slash path/types/lib references, or @ts-check invalidates reuse because those fields drive the loader's file/resolution graph and cannot be recomputed by a plain clone."
  }
],
"flow": "The path begins in ProjectCollectionBuilder.markFilesChanged (tsc/internal/project/projectcollectionbuilder.go:1543), which sets p.dirtyFilePath only when the changed set collapses to one file contained in the project; deletions, package.json edits, newly seen/created paths, or multiple distinct paths clear it to force a full rebuild. Project.CreateProgram then sees dirtyFilePath != \"\", an existing Program, and an unchanged command line, and calls compiler.Program.UpdateProgram (tsc/internal/compiler/program.go:323). UpdateProgram delegates to ReuseProgram; if ReuseProgram returns reused=true it forwards that program and the acquired changed file, otherwise it constructs a fresh NewProgram but still returns the speculatively parsed file. ReuseProgram first acquires the new file through the host (parse cache Acquire or content-mapper transform) and immediately rejects the optimization when the path participates in package redirection (redirectFilesByPath/redirectTargetsMap), when there is a module-resolution error, when canReplaceFileInProgram fails, when either version needs synthetic importHelpers/tslib or JSX-runtime imports, or when supplemental files differ. Only if all checks pass does it clone the Program shell, reusing processedFiles/resolutionData/unresolvedImports/knownSymlinks/packageNames, swap the changed entry in files and filesByPath, and initialize a new checker pool. Package redirects must rebuild because the same package can be installed at multiple node_modules locations and the redirect target/identity is recomputed during load, which a clone cannot do; synthetic imports (importHelpers, JSX runtime) must rebuild because the per-file synthetic-import specifier maps (importHelpersImportSpecifiers/jsxRuntimeImportSpecifiers) are not recomputed by cloning. The predicate invalidates any change to import specifier text/order or resolution mode, module augmentation/ambient module names, referenced/type-reference/lib-reference directives, or the @ts-check directive, since these change the structure of the program's file/resolution graph. Ownership is refcounted: CreateProgram notes that UpdateProgram already acquired the changed file, so on success it Refs (parseCache/contentMappedParseCache) every other source file and duplicate that the cloned program now references; on fallback it Derefs the changed file's speculative acquire so the rebuilt program, which re-acquires everything through the host, is its sole owner. Thus a success transfers ownership of the changed file plus new references to all unchanged parsed files, while a fallback releases the speculative acquire and leaves the full rebuild's acquisitions as the only owners. UpdateProgram itself never mutates caches; the refcounting contract is enforced by the project layer using the exact dirtyFile pointer returned."
}

{
  "findings": [
    {
      "path": "tsc/internal/tsoptions/tsconfigparsing.go",
      "symbol": "parseConfig",
      "line": 1082,
      "explanation": "Recursive core of config inheritance. It first normalizes basePath, converts configFileName to a path and checks the active resolutionStack; if the resolved path is already on the stack it emits diagnostics.Circularity_detected_while_resolving_configuration_Colon_0 and returns the raw config without descending further (lines 1091-1105). It parses the project's own raw JSON via parseOwnConfigOfJson/parseOwnConfigOfJsonSourceFile, and stores PathsBasePath on ownConfig.options when a 'paths' mapping exists so that relative path mappings inherited from a base in another directory retain their origin (lines 1115-1121). Its applyExtendedConfig closure (1123-1179) resolves each base through getExtendedConfig, then, for 'include'/'exclude'/'files', rebases inherited relative specs by computing relativeDifference = relative(extended config dir, current basePath) and CombinePaths(relativeDifference, spec) unless the spec is rooted or uses the ${configDir} template (1128-1163). It also inherits contentMappers and compileOnSave when locally absent and merges the base's compiler options. For ownConfig.extendedConfigPath it pushes resolvedPath onto a copied resolutionStack and applies every base, supporting either a single string or an ordered []string (diamond-safe) (1181-1193). Precedence is handled by writing inherited values into ownConfig.raw only for properties the local config lacks (1194-1215) and by mergeCompilerOptions(result.options, ownConfig.options, ownConfig.raw), so local settings override inherited ones."
    },
    {
      "path": "tsc/internal/tsoptions/tsconfigparsing.go",
      "symbol": "getExtendedConfig",
      "line": 1015,
      "explanation": "Fetches a resolved base config for one 'extends' entry, consulting the optional ExtendedConfigCache only when safe. It converts extendedConfigFileName to a canonical path and then, if extendedConfigCache != nil and the path is not already in resolutionStack, delegates to extendedConfigCache.GetExtendedConfig; otherwise it calls ParseExtendedConfig directly (1024-1035). The resolutionStack guard deliberately bypasses the cache when a cycle is detected, because cache entries are locked during parsing and re-entering the same entry from the same goroutine would deadlock; parseConfig is then left to report the circularity error (comment 1027-1030). It propagates the entry's errors and, when a sourceFile is present, records the base's own and transitively extended source files into result.extendedSourceFiles (1041-1048), returning cacheEntry.extendedConfig and any errors."
    },
    {
      "path": "tsc/internal/tsoptions/tsconfigparsing.go",
      "symbol": "getFileNamesFromConfigSpecs",
      "line": 1934,
      "explanation": "Expands validated file specs into the final ordered file list, returning the files plus the count of literal (explicit) file names. Explicit 'files' entries are resolved to absolute paths and always placed in literalFileMap verbatim; include/exclude can never remove them (1962-1967). Wildcard 'include' specs are expanded via vfsmatch.ReadDirectory over basePath using supportedExtensionsWithJsonIfResolveJsonModule and validatedExcludeSpecs (1971) into wildcardFileMap. Competing extensions are resolved with extension-priority rules: hasFileWithHigherPriorityExtension skips a wildcard/literal file when a same-name higher-priority extension was already included, and removeWildcardFilesWithLowerPriorityExtension prunes lower-priority wildcard entries, including the legacy .d.ts vs .js/.jsx behavior (1875-1925, 1996-2010). JSON files get distinct treatment: a wildcard *.json match is stored separately in wildCardJsonFileMap only if there is an include spec ending in .json that matches via jsonOnlyIncludeMatchers, and it skips the extension-priority path (1969-1989). The result concatenates literal files, then wildcard files, then JSON wildcard files (2013-2023)."
    }
  ],
  "flow": "The top-level entry parseJsonConfigFileContentWorker (tsc/internal/tsoptions/tsconfigparsing.go:1237) normalizes basePathForFileNames, calls parseConfig (line 1258) to build the merged configuration, then validates the raw 'files'/'include'/'exclude' specs with validateSpecs and writes a configFileSpecs value onto the source file (1265-1377). Finally it calls getFileNames (1460), which invokes getFileNamesFromConfigSpecs (line 1462) to expand specs into the concrete, ordered file list carried on ParsedCommandLine. So the three stages are: combine configs recursively (parseConfig) -> resolve each inherited layer (getExtendedConfig, quirk of the cache) -> expand validated specs into files (getFileNamesFromConfigSpecs).\n\nInheritance/precedence: parseConfig recurses because each base is obtained by getExtendedConfig, which either hits the cache or calls ParseExtendedConfig, which in turn calls parseConfig again (line 1075), building the base's own merged config before returning. A config may list multiple bases (ownConfig.extendedConfigPath as a string or []string); they are applied in array order into a shared extendsResult, with later bases merging over earlier ones via mergeCompilerOptions(result.options, extendedConfig.options, extendsRaw). Local settings always win: inherited include/exclude/files are only copied into the local raw map when the local config didn't specify that property (1130-1132, 1194-1202), and own compiler options are merged last with mergeCompilerOptions(result.options, ownConfig.options, ownConfig.raw) so they override all bases. Existing options passed into the worker are merged underneath as well (1259).\n\nOrigin of relative mappings: when local options contain 'paths', parseConfig records PathsBasePath = basePath (1115-1121) so that a 'paths' map inherited from a base in a different directory is still resolved relative to the directory of the config that actually declared it; inherited include/exclude/files are similarly rebased via the relativeDifference between the base's directory and the extending config's basePath (1143-1152), preserving the correct origin instead of resolving against the wrong directory.\n\nCircular inheritance: parseConfig detects a cycle by testing slices.Contains(resolutionStack, resolvedPath) and emits Circularity_detected_while_resolving_configuration_Colon_0 without recursing. Before descending, it copies the stack (resolutionStack = append(resolutionStack, resolvedPath), 1183) so diamond-shaped extends graphs don't pollute sibling branches. getExtendedConfig additionally guards the cache: when the target path is already on the resolution stack it bypasses the cache and calls ParseExtendedConfig directly, because cache entries lock during parsing and re-locking from the same goroutine would deadlock; parseConfig then reports the circularity.\n\nFile selection differences: explicit 'files' entries are normalized and unconditionally included in literalFileMap and can never be excluded. Wildcard 'include' matches are gathered through directory enumeration minus excludes, then filtered by extension priority: a file is skipped if a higher-priority same-name extension is already present, and lower-priority wildcard siblings are removed. JSON wildcard matches are treated separately, only admitted when a specifically '.json'-suffixed include spec matches them, and stored in wildCardJsonFileMap; they bypass the TS/JS extension-priority logic entirely. The final list is literal files first, then non-JSON wildcard files, then JSON wildcard files, with the literal count returned alongside."
}

{
  "findings": [
    {
      "path": "tsc/internal/parser/parser.go",
      "symbol": "tryParseTypeArgumentsInExpression",
      "line": 5301,
      "explanation": "The speculative driver. It first bails out cheaply when p.contextFlags has ast.NodeFlagsJavaScriptFile set or the current token is neither KindLessThanToken nor KindLessThanLessThanToken (parser.go:5306). Otherwise it saves state via p.mark(), rescans '<' with p.reScanLessThanToken() to split a lexed '<<' into two '<' tokens (parser.go:5310), consumes '<' with nextToken, parses a comma-separated type list through p.parseDelimitedList(PCTypeArguments, parseType) (parser.go:5312), then rescans the closer via p.reScanGreaterThanToken() to split a merged '>>'/'>>>'/'>>=' back into a lone '>' (parser.go:5314). Only if a closing KindGreaterThanToken is found does it consume it and consult p.canFollowTypeArgumentsInExpression() (parser.go:5320). If the predicate favors type arguments it returns the NodeList, committing to the interpretation; otherwise control falls through to p.rewind(state) (parser.go:5325) and it returns nil, abandoning the attempt so relational/shift parsing can resume from the saved position."
    },
    {
      "path": "tsc/internal/parser/parser.go",
      "symbol": "canFollowTypeArgumentsInExpression",
      "line": 5329,
      "explanation": "The lookahead predicate that judges the token immediately after the closing '>'. It returns true outright when the next token is KindOpenParenToken (call like f<number>(42)), KindNoSubstitutionTemplateLiteral or KindTemplateHead (tagged template f<T>`...`), which strongly imply a type-argument list (parser.go:5335). It returns false for KindLessThanToken, KindGreaterThanToken, KindPlusToken and KindMinusToken because a following '<' is nonsensical, a following '>' is ambiguous with a re-scanned '>>' shift operator, and in this position '+'/'-' are unary rather than binary operators (parser.go:5340). In all other cases it returns true when there is a preceding line break (hasPrecedingLineBreak), when the current token is a binary operator (isBinaryOperator), or when it cannot start an expression (!isStartOfExpression) (parser.go:5345); otherwise the tokens look like operands of a relational/shift expression and the type-argument reading is rejected."
    },
